# The permission to delete Time Machine's local snapshots without a password: ONE rule for ONE exact command,
# /usr/bin/tmutil thinlocalsnapshots / 999999999999 4 (deletes the local snapshots on this Mac and nothing else).
# The arguments must match exactly, so this rule CANNOT:
#   - delete backups on the Time Machine disk   (tmutil delete and the other verbs are not allowed)
#   - turn Time Machine off                     (tmutil disable is not allowed)
#   - run any other program or any other tmutil command line
# It does let any program running as that account delete the local snapshots without asking. Local snapshots can be
# the only copies of recent changes while the backup disk is not connected, and cannot be brought back.
#
# __UID__ becomes "#<the account's numeric user ID>" when installed: a user ID, never a name, so no account name is
# written into a file root reads. Each account has its own file, /etc/sudoers.d/tmutil-thin-<user ID>.
#
# Install:  Mac Analyzers > Storage > Time Machine > Allow…   (the macOS administrator prompt), or in Terminal, as
#           yourself:  ./storage-analyzer/storage-manage-agents.sh sudoers-install   (validates with visudo first)
# Remove:   Time Machine > Options > Remove Permission…, or  ./storage-analyzer/storage-manage-agents.sh sudoers-uninstall
__UID__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tmutil thinlocalsnapshots / 999999999999 4
